WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.820
I managed to find a copy of Neil deGrasse
Tyson's PhD thesis.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.610
And I spent all week trying to understand
it.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.110
But it wasn't easy to read because it's 370
pages long.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:17.599
And theses are not intended to be read by
anyone but experts on the subject.

00:00:17.599 --> 00:00:21.099
So I became an expert on galaxies.

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:27.239
Obtaining a PhD is the highest academic qualification
for someone wanting to pursue a career in

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:28.599
science.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:34.160
It's been a few years now since I left my
own PhD to pursue other things.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.620
Looking at Tyson's success, I thought that
his career path might have been more of a

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.010
straight line to get to such a level.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.700
So imagine my surprise when I read that this
thesis was not his first attempt at a PhD.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.670
His line was indeed more squiggly.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:56.429
He started as a graduate student at the University
of Texas, but felt that the expectation to

00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:01.519
dedicate all of his time to research meant
that he had to give up his other interests

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:03.710
and the rest of who he was.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.320
NDT: "And some of the advisors, some of the
other advisors, thinking they're doing me

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.880
a favour said, you know, you have this personality,
why don't you become a computer salesman.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.549
I know some people who work for computers,
you could be-.

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:17.740
They're to try to do me a favour by getting
me out of their community.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.009
I said to myself, if I'm going to have to
do a whole new PhD, it's not going to be at

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:23.009
this institution.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:25.720
If it's got to be fresh, it will be fresh
in another place."

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.070
I wanted to read his thesis to see what kind
of scientific foundation he had before launching

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:38.210
himself into a career in science communication,
to see for myself his contributions to scientific

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:44.690
research, and to test myself to see if I stand
a chance of understanding any of it.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:50.360
For his second attempt at a PhD this time
at Columbia University, Tyson took a stab

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:56.189
at understanding why the centre of our galaxy
is the way it is.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:02.649
His thesis has the title, A Study of the Abundance
Distributions Along the Minor Axis of the

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:03.829
Galactic Bulge.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:07.260
It was published in 1992, when he was 33.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:14.010
A PhD thesis or dissertation is an account
of years of intense scientific research, and

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.190
it must contain an original contribution to
human knowledge.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.170
So what was Tyson's?

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.210
Well, let's have a look.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:29.780
Chapter one opens with the quote, "It is important
not to worship what is known, but to question

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.110
it".

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:39.110
Tyson then describes the galactic bulge as
a remarkable, compact zoo of stellar populations.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.560
Let's draw a map of our galaxy the Milky Way.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:48.130
This is a side-on view with many stars in
the spiral arms of the disk.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:55.319
Around the Galactic Centre, there is a bulge
with a high concentration of stars, this is

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:57.159
Tyson's Zoo.

00:02:57.159 --> 00:03:03.740
Perhaps the main attraction is a black hole
at the very centre, astronomers can peer in

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:09.190
at the black hole, our galaxy's fierce lion,
but only from afar.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:16.099
There's a fear of being eaten if we ever got
too close, but the sun and therefore our solar

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:23.290
system and us are out here, nice and safe
held by the spiral arms.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:30.030
Out there we have the halo comprised of older
stars and globular clusters, tightly bound

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.010
groups of many stars.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.741
Here’s Tyson’s sketch of what he did,
which is that he looked into the galactic

00:03:37.741 --> 00:03:44.020
bulge along seven different lines of sight
to understand the populations of stars in

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.490
the zoo and how they change as you get further
from the center.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:54.910
The chosen lines of sight are low extinction
windows through the plane of the disk.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:04:00.599
So he looked through sections of space where
there isn’t so much dust and gas in the

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:02.959
way to obscure the stars.

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:09.500
These are images of the seven windows and
you can notice the steep drop in star density

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.349
from the inner-most bulge to the outer-most
halo.

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:18.680
Each window is named with it’s latitude,
the smaller numbers are looking further out

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.320
towards the sparse halo.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.820
Tyson measured properties of these stars like
their chemical abundance and velocities to

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:32.290
answer the following questions: What is the
abundance distribution function at different

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.110
galactic latitudes within the bulge?

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:40.980
Is there a smooth transition in abundance
between the bulge and the spheroid (halo)?

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.070
If not, at what latitude does the transition
occur?

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:52.470
And, was the bulge’s primordial gas used
completely in star formation or was it expelled

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.450
by ongoing supernova-driven winds?

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:03.420
To measure the chemical abundance of the stars,
Tyson used the Cerro Tololo Inter-american

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.570
Observatory located in Chile.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:11.100
To get time observing on the telescope he
would have had to submit a proposal maybe

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:17.650
even a year in advance and compete against
all the other astronomers wanting a turn.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:24.160
Then he had to hope that on his allocated
days, the sky wouldn’t be cloudy because

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.790
if it is, he wont see anything.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:33.660
Chile was chosen to house many observatories
because it’s dry and with little light pollution,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.410
but every observing run is still a roll of
the die.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:44.850
Tyson’s first observing run was for one
night on the 7th of June 1989.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:54.500
To get to the telescopes is a long drive up
a bumpy, scary road to an altitude of 2200m.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.400
Up there the oxygen level is so low that you
might be out of breath just walking around.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.280
So why would he go all that way just for one
night?

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.980
Perhaps he hoped to observe for longer but
got bad weather.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:16.430
All I can tell from his thesis is that he
observed 4 fields of stars that night and

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.810
returned a year later for 2 more nights to
get the rest.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:26.250
Tyson collected information about his stars
in the bulge and processed that data to find

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:32.880
their colours, temperatures, magnitudes and
also their chemical compositions.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.190
He used the Washington photometric system
to measure certain wavelengths of starlight

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.810
and look for absorption lines.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:46.681
These lines indicate that some of the light
that left the hot interior of the star, was

00:06:46.681 --> 00:06:52.400
absorbed by atoms/ions in the star’s outer
atmosphere.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.800
Some photons will be blocked from reaching
us and their absence reveals what elements

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.570
the stars are made of.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.980
It’s not all smooth sailing though.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:06.091
There are plenty of errors in every step of
the process to account for.

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:13.690
For Tyson these included overcrowding of stars
in his inner windows causing some of the light

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.220
profiles to overlap.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:21.060
There was also the difficult job of removing
foreground stars from the images.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.320
These both gave uncertainties in the final
measurements.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:32.980
There are many pages of data, math and nuanced
details but Tyson eventually arrived at abundance

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:37.550
distributions which he said is the principal
focus of the study.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:43.220
Doing science means making incremental changes
to models of how things work.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:49.430
Tyson noted some existing models of how galaxies
chemically evolve and wanted to see if his

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:54.580
newly collected data from the bulge fit any
of these models.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:08:01.060
The simplest model starts off with a ball
of gas, and all it can do is turn into stars.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.110
No gas escapes.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:09.490
This is called the Closed Box model, and if
you were to plot the metal content of stars

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:15.680
against the number of stars you would get
a plot shaped like this.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.500
This is the abundance distribution.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.460
In astronomy a metal is any element that's
not Hydrogen or Helium.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:30.890
That’s not how chemists would define metals
but in astronomy it allows an easy distinction

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:38.330
between elements (H and He) that have existed
since the birth of the universe, and heavier

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:42.320
elements that must have been made by stars.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:49.800
Stars make metals through nuclear fusion in
their cores or in supernova explosions.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:56.460
Supernovae release metals back into the interstellar
medium, the space between the stars, from

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:01.350
which new stars with higher metallicities
may form.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:09.250
A variant of the closed box is the leaky box
which allows some gas to escape into the rest

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.370
of the galaxy.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.770
The abundance distribution for the leaky box
would be the same shape but shifted to the

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:16.770
left.

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:23.700
The stars have less metal in them on average
than stars in a closed box because those metal-rich

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.910
gas explosions from supernova are escaping.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:31.380
So let’s take a look at Tyson’s abundance
distributions.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:37.010
Here’s the first of his seven fields, the
one closest to the galactic center.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:44.210
He measures metallicity as the ratio of Iron
to Hydrogen and says there is remarkable agreement

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:50.390
with the abundance distribution expected from
the simple model/closed box.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:57.340
He fits a theoretical curve with a yield of
1.25 which is a measure of how much metal

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:01.470
is being returned to the interstellar medium.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:08.820
This is scaled to the metallicity of the sun
so zero would be the sun and one is ten times

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.470
the sun’s abundance.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:16.840
Going further from the center his next plot
has the same shape but shifted slightly to

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:17.840
the right.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:24.340
The error bars are quite large and he says
this also fits the simple model but with a

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.100
higher yield.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:31.520
The third plot moving further out again has
a higher average abundance but he concludes

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:40.320
that so far the region from the galactic center
to 0.75 kiloparsecs away is a well-mixed closed

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:41.320
box.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:47.530
A parsec is a unit of distance but if you’re
more familiar with lightyears then this closed

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:52.580
box region is about 5000 lightyears across.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:57.590
The next few fields show a shift to the left
and drop in average abundance.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:03.920
We might be seeing that the box is leaking
or there's a more complicated process at hand.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.720
But there are also not many stars being measured
here.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:12.160
The last field, out in the halo, only has
3 stars!

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:13.160
This is them.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:18.510
So he doesn’t really have a lot of statistics
on his side here.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:23.590
Tyson’s measurements constitute the first
direct measurement of the galactic latitude

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:27.840
dependence of [Fe/H] in the galactic bulge.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.630
He shows that it initially increases away
from the galactic center then steeply drops

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.450
off as you leave the bulge.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:42.190
This drop off occurs around 1 kiloparsec away
from the center leading him to conclude that

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:47.390
those stars within 1 kiloparsec followed a
common evolution.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:54.100
He also concludes that the inner bulge was
not significantly enriched by infall of gas

00:11:54.100 --> 00:12:01.450
and that the inner bulge underwent no catastrophic
mass-loss from supernova-driven winds.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.780
He finds that the inner bulge is chemically
distinct from the halo while the transition

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:09.780
region is a blending of the two.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:14.910
Astrophysicists care about these questions
because any new information about the state

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:21.530
of our galaxy helps to refine all the models
which try to explain what our galaxy is and

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:23.160
how it got here.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:28.560
Tyson talks about the implications of his
findings for models of how clouds of gas may

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.480
have collapsed to form our galaxy in the first
place.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:37.810
There has been a lot more work done on galactic
evolution since this was written in the 90s.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:44.740
Large scale surveys done by satellite and
ground based telescopes like GAIA and APOGEE

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:51.220
have been able to create maps of millions
of stars and dwarf the three data points Tyson

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.550
had in the halo.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:13:00.330
Astronomy moves pretty fast since every year
there are upgraded tools and methods of observing.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:07.029
This thesis wasn’t Tyson’s only contribution
to research, he has 13 research papers which

00:13:07.029 --> 00:13:10.250
all will have contributed some new form of
understanding.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:18.180
A few of his papers are taken from chapters
of this thesis and i’ll link those in the

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:24.270
description since the entire thesis as a whole
can only be found through the University of

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:25.270
Columbia.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:31.490
His combined work has racked up a bit over
2000 citations, instances where other scientists

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.610
used it in some way.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:38.540
Whilst this isn’t extremely high, it’s
pretty clear that Neil Degrasse Tyson’s

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:44.140
main contribution to science is not in his
research but in his work as a communicator.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:50.520
His early research was his introduction to
all the key workings of the subject.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:55.310
We can also take a look at his acknowledgments
page, he thanked the scientists who helped

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.660
them along the way.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:01.540
He thanked his supervisor Mike Rich for his
encouragement and attention to scientific

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:05.440
detail and dedicated the work to his family.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.529
He ended his thesis with a final thought.

00:14:08.529 --> 00:14:13.570
I have lived and fulfilled a dream, yet my
life is just beginning.

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:19.270
Most of the notes I took and the facts I learned
about galaxies never made it to the screen

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:20.270
in this video.

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:25.990
But I really feel that for this week at least,
I did become an expert on galaxies.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.240
Even though I might forget it all next week.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:34.160
I found this thesis surprisingly good at covering
a base of topics that one would need going

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.279
forwards to work in astronomy or its communication.

00:14:37.279 --> 00:14:42.250
And I suppose that's exactly the point of
a thesis.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:47.050
But then success is all about what you do
from here.

